[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Exception Handling in PHP (try, catch, finally)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Exception handling is a crucial aspect of writing robust and maintainable PHP code. It allows you to gracefully handle unexpected errors and prevent your application from crashing. PHP provides the 'try', 'catch', and 'finally' blocks to manage exceptions effectively."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The 'try' Block"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'try' block encloses the code that might potentially throw an exception. If an exception occurs within the 'try' block, the execution jumps to the corresponding 'catch' block."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"<?php
 try {
  // Code that might throw an exception
  $result = 10 / 0; // This will cause a division by zero error
  echo "Result: " . $result;
 } catch (Exception $e) {
  // Code to handle the exception
  echo 'Caught exception: ' .  $e->getMessage() . "\n";
 }
?>"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"The 'catch' Block"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'catch' block is used to handle exceptions that are thrown within the 'try' block. You can have multiple 'catch' blocks to handle different types of exceptions. The 'catch' block specifies the type of exception it can handle (e.g., 'Exception', 'TypeError', 'DivisionByZeroError')."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"<?php
 try {
  // Code that might throw an exception
  throw new Exception('This is a custom exception.');
 } catch (Exception $e) {
  // Code to handle the exception
  echo 'Caught exception: ' .  $e->getMessage() . "\n";
 }
?>"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"The 'finally' Block"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'finally' block is executed regardless of whether an exception was thrown or caught. It's typically used to perform cleanup operations, such as closing database connections or releasing resources. The 'finally' block is optional."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"<?php
 try {
  // Code that might throw an exception
  $result = 10 / 2;
  echo "Result: " . $result . "\n";
 } catch (Exception $e) {
  // Code to handle the exception
  echo 'Caught exception: ' .  $e->getMessage() . "\n";
 } finally {
  // Code that always runs
  echo "Finally block executed.\n";
 }
?>"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Exception Hierarchy"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"PHP has a built-in exception hierarchy. All exceptions inherit from the base 'Exception' class. You can create your own custom exception classes by extending the 'Exception' class or one of its subclasses."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"<?php
 class MyCustomException extends Exception {
  public function __toString() {
   return __CLASS__ . ": [" . $this->code . "]: " . $this->message . "\n";
  }
 }

 try {
  throw new MyCustomException('This is my custom exception', 42);
 } catch (MyCustomException $e) {
  echo $e;
 } catch (Exception $e) {
  echo "Caught generic exception: " . $e->getMessage() . "\n";
 }
?>"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Be specific with your exception types in 'catch' blocks to handle different errors appropriately."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the 'finally' block for essential cleanup operations."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid catching exceptions that you cannot handle properly. Re-throw them or log them for later investigation."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Create custom exception classes to represent specific error conditions in your application."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"By following these guidelines, you can write more reliable and maintainable PHP code that gracefully handles errors and prevents unexpected crashes."}]
